WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.069 --> 00:00:02.730
 "Let's start with the end
of the world, why don't we?

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:06.630
Get it over with and move on
to more interesting things."

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:09.480
Those first two sentences
introduce the fact

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.680
that the end of the
world is happening,

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:14.565
which I think of as a really
important inciting incident.

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:21.320
You aren't stuck in the past
when you're writing fantasy.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:26.910
You aren't stuck in space when
you're writing science fiction.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.650
A lot of the things
that we traditionally

00:00:28.650 --> 00:00:32.729
think of as science fiction
are actually fantasy.

00:00:32.729 --> 00:00:34.560
For example, "Star Wars."

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:35.770
What's the force?

00:00:35.770 --> 00:00:37.020
You can call it magic.

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.450
It's space magic.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:43.440
In this MasterClass, we'll talk
about how to develop characters

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:44.400
that are interesting.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:47.310
People are 3-dimensional,
people are contradictory.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:49.560
I'll be using my own
techniques derived

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.238
from psychology and my own
background as a psychologist.

00:00:52.238 --> 00:00:54.780
We're going to actually start
going through an exercise where

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.710
we build a world from the planet
level on down to the people.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:01.350
We will talk about how to
become a professional writer.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:04.709
If you work and hone your craft,
you will eventually break in.

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.330
When I was small, I
would go to the library

00:01:10.330 --> 00:01:13.210
and I would ask the librarians
to recommend science fiction

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:13.950
books.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:17.140
They would recommend the
big well-known names.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:20.650
The problem with this is,
as a young Black girl,

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:22.930
reading a bunch
of novels written

00:01:22.930 --> 00:01:25.522
by a bunch of
middle-aged white dudes,

00:01:25.522 --> 00:01:27.230
there wasn't a whole
lot in those stories

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:30.110
that was speaking to me.

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:33.320
If you, like me, are
a marginalized writer,

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:35.660
you're going to
encounter some bigotry.

00:01:35.660 --> 00:01:39.020
There are people out there
who need your example, who

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:42.540
need you to go forth
and fight that battle,

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.210
so they won't have
to fight as hard.

00:01:44.210 --> 00:01:45.350
And it's worth it.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:50.000
You help to change the
profession by your existence.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:54.220
I'm N. K. Jemisin, and
this is my MasterClass.

